    {"problem": "The AC runs all afternoon and the house still sits at 26°C", "solution": "A low refrigerant charge, a dirty coil or a unit that was undersized from day one are the usual suspects. We test charge and airflow first. If the unit still runs on R-22, a refrigerant being phased out and costly to top up, replacing it is usually the smarter spend."},
    {"problem": "The sunroom is useless in January and again in July", "solution": "Glass rooms lose heat fast, gain it fast, and are rarely on the main ducts. A single-zone ductless mini split heats and cools the room on its own, so you can finally use it year round."},
    {"problem": "The basement feels damp and chilly with the furnace running", "solution": "Lakeshore dampness and a basement with one or two supply registers make a cold, clammy space. We look at returns and airflow, and a ductless unit can add heat, cooling and dehumidification just for that level."},
    {"problem": "The furnace is the one that came with the house", "solution": "A furnace from the 1970s or 1990s is often a single-stage, mid-efficiency unit near the end of its working life. A two-stage, variable-speed model at 96% to 97% AFUE burns less gas and holds a steadier temperature, from $3,300 installed."},
